                    fn map_set(m: u32, index: u32, key: u32, value: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 22
                    fn map_key(m: u32, index: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 23
                    fn map_val(m: u32, index: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 24
                    fn map_len(m: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 25
                    /// ── Reuse / FBIP (Perceus reset + reuse, indices 26–28). The in-place update story: when an
                    ///    `rc==1` value is consumed and a same-arity value is built in the same breath (`List.map`,
                    ///    record update, a functional cons rebuild), hand the dying node's shell straight to the new
                    ///    allocation instead of free→malloc. This is Koka/Lean's core perf win, made frame-limited by
                    ///    the P3/P4 rule below: reuse fires ONLY when the node is UNIQUE (`rc==1`), so peak heap can
                    ///    never grow — a reused shell is memory that was already live and is about to die.
                    ///
                    ///    `reset(node) -> u32` — the drop-to-token op. If `node` is UNIQUE (`rc==1`): drop the
                    ///    children it owns (a normal cascading drop of each child reference), then RETAIN the emptied
                    ///    shell and return it as a non-null REUSE TOKEN (the same handle, now childless, rc still 1).
                    ///    If `node` is SHARED (`rc>1`): decrement and return 0 (null token) — no reuse, the other
                    ///    owners keep the node intact. A null token is always safe to pass on: the reuse-constructors
                    ///    treat it as "no cell to reuse" and allocate fresh. So the compiler emits `reset` on the
                    ///    scrutinee/old value and threads the token into the constructor that rebuilds it.
                    ///
                    ///    `arr-alloc-reuse(len, token) -> u32` — like `arr-alloc(len)`, but if `token` is a non-null
                    ///    reuse token, refit that shell to `len` NULL slots and return it (no allocation); else
                    ///    allocate fresh. `sum-new-reuse(disc, payload, token)` — like `sum-new`, reusing `token`'s
                    ///    shell for the new `(disc, payload)` node when non-null. Passing token 0 makes both behave
                    ///    exactly as their non-reuse forms, so a reset that declined to yield a token is transparent.
                    fn reset(node: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 26
                    fn arr_alloc_reuse(len: u32, token: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 27
                    fn sum_new_reuse(disc: u32, payload: u32, token: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 28
                    /// ── List representation: a 32-way radix trie (indices 29–33) — an IMMUTABLE, structurally-shared
                    ///    growable sequence. This is the runtime representation of the language's LIST type: a list is
                    ///    runtime-length and grows/updates functionally (`List.push`/`List.update`) with O(log₃₂ N)
                    ///    push/get/update and path-copying sharing, which the flat `arr` (fixed-arity, for tuple/record)
                    ///    cannot do. There is NO separate `Vec` type in the language — a list built as a `(list …)`
                    ///    literal and a list grown by push are the SAME type over THIS representation; which structure
                    ///    backs a list is unobservable (a list renders `(list …)` regardless), so the runtime is free to
                    ///    use the flat array for a small/literal list or this trie for a grown one. The representation is
                    ///    a pure runtime concern (see lib.rs § "Persistent vector"): these five signatures are the frozen
                    ///    contract; a later switch to a tail-optimized trie or an RRB tree keeps them byte-identical.
                    ///
                    ///    Ownership (value-heap-runtime.md §Constructors Consume And Accessors Borrow): `vec-empty` produces a new owned list.
                    ///    `vec-push`/`vec-update` are CONSTRUCTORS — they CONSUME the input list and the element and
                    ///    produce a NEW owned list; the old version is untouched (persistence), so a caller keeping
                    ///    both versions `dup`s the input first. `vec-get` BORROWS (rc unchanged; the list still owns
                    ///    the element). `vec-len` returns a `u32` by value. An out-of-bounds `vec-get`/`vec-update`
                    ///    index TRAPS (fail-fast, like `arr-get`; the compiler emits the bounds check on its side).
                    fn vec_empty() -> u32;
                    /// 29 — a new empty vector
                    fn vec_len(v: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 30 — element count
                    fn vec_get(v: u32, index: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 31 — element at index (borrowed)
                    fn vec_push(v: u32, elem: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 32 — v with elem appended (consumes both)
                    fn vec_update(v: u32, index: u32, elem: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 33 — v with index set (consumes both)
                    /// ── Bytes rope (indices 34–36) — O(1) concat/slice over SHARED byte leaves. A Bytes value becomes
                    ///    a rope: a tree of concat/slice nodes bottoming out in the existing byte leaves (13–16), so
                    ///    `bytes-concat`/`bytes-slice` allocate one node and copy NO bytes — killing the O(n²) copy
                    ///    cascade a compiler hits assembling a wasm module by concatenating encoded sections
                    ///    (value-heap-runtime.md §Deferred Materialization Is Permitted Behind The Observable Bytes). The representation is a pure
                    ///    runtime concern: it reuses the existing tagless node (`handles.len()` ∈ {0,1,2} selects
                    ///    leaf/slice/concat — see lib.rs § "Bytes rope"), so 13–16 are byte-unchanged and a rope is
                    ///    INDISTINGUISHABLE from a flat buffer by `bytes-len`/`bytes-get`/equality (a rope node
                    ///    flattens to a leaf on first full read — unobservable per memory-and-resource-model.md
                    ///    #Sharing Is Not Observable). A later balancing/tail change keeps these three signatures
                    ///    byte-identical.
                    ///
                    ///    Ownership (value-heap-runtime.md §Constructors Consume And Accessors Borrow): all three are CONSTRUCTORS — they CONSUME
                    ///    their Bytes operand(s) (stored in the new node's `handles` without dup, like `arr-set`) and
                    ///    produce a NEW owned Bytes; a caller keeping an operand `dup`s it first. `bytes-concat` is the
                    ///    bytes of `a` then `b` (empty is the identity). `bytes-slice(buf, start, len)` is `len` bytes
                    ///    from `start`, total-or-trap (`start + len > bytes-len(buf)` traps; `len == 0` is empty, never
                    ///    a trap). `bytes-compact(buf)` returns a Bytes equal by content whose storage is INDEPENDENT
                    ///    of any larger buffer `buf` was sliced from (memory-and-resource-model.md #Retained Storage) —
                    ///    a slice that pinned a huge parent releases it. `bytes-set` is unchanged: it targets only a
                    ///    freshly `bytes-alloc`'d leaf, never a rope node.
                    fn bytes_concat(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 34 — bytes of a then b (consumes both)
                    fn bytes_slice(buf: u32, start: u32, len: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 35 — len bytes from start (consumes buf)
                    fn bytes_compact(buf: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 36 — content-equal, storage-independent
                    /// ── Map (CHAMP, indices 37–45) — the REAL persistent key→value map, replacing the positional
                    ///    stub (21–25, now vestigial). Internally a Compressed Hash-Array Mapped Prefix tree over the
                    ///    SAME tagless node (no new field): a node's `raw` holds [datamap:u32][nodemap:u32][size:u32]
                    ///    and its `handles` hold data entries (2/entry: k,v) then subnodes, in bit-position order. Keys
                    ///    cross as PLAIN handles; the runtime hashes + STRUCTURALLY compares them (tag-free — no
                    ///    serialization, no upcall, no reentrancy). Correct because keys within one map are homogeneous
                    ///    (one key type/map; cross-type = compile-time reject) and every value form is canonical modulo
                    ///    Bytes-rope compaction (the compiler emits `bytes-compact` on a Bytes key before these ops).
                    ///    Iteration is deterministic (hash order); the compiler owns the canonical byte-form sort.
                    fn map_empty() -> u32;
                    /// 37 — the canonical empty map
                    fn map_insert(m: u32, key: u32, val: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 38 — m with key↦val (consumes m, key, val)
                    fn map_lookup(m: u32, key: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 39 — val, or NULL if absent (borrows m + key)
                    fn map_remove(m: u32, key: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 40 — m without key (consumes m; borrows key)
                    fn map_size(m: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 41 — entry count (O(1), from the root's size)
                    /// ── Map cursor (indices 42–45) — non-allocating iteration (rendering, equality, folds). A cursor
                    ///    is a STATELESS value: `map-iter-next` CONSUMES the cursor and returns the advanced one — at
                    ///    rc==1 it refits in place (FBIP, zero steady-state alloc), at rc>1 it path-copies (forkable /
                    ///    peekable / tee for free). `map-iter` borrows m (dups its root to keep the tree live for the
                    ///    walk); the cursor OWNS its descent-path frames, so the existing iterative `op_drop` reclaims
                    ///    it with no special-casing. `map-iter-key` returns NULL when exhausted (a key is never NULL);
                    ///    the projections BORROW (a kept key/val escapes the loop via a compiler-emitted `dup`, like
                    ///    `arr-get`). Static dispatch: the compiler emits map- vs set-iter directly (no cursor tag).
                    fn map_iter(m: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 42 — cursor at the first entry (or exhausted)
                    fn map_iter_next(cur: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 43 — advance (consumes cur, returns new cursor)
                    fn map_iter_key(cur: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 44 — current key, or NULL if exhausted (borrows)
                    fn map_iter_val(cur: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 45 — current value (borrows)
                    /// ── Set (CHAMP, indices 46–53) — the CHAMP minus the value column: entries are 1 handle, not 2.
                    ///    Same trie core, hashing, structural comparison, and RC as the map (a shared Rust core behind
                    ///    the distinct `set-*` ops — the economy does not leak across the seam). A Set is a PRIMITIVE
                    ///    collection, not a Map<T,Unit>. `set-contains` is a TOTAL predicate (no positional access;
                    ///    sets are unordered). Mirrors the map ops; the cursor obeys the same stateless/FBIP contract.
                    fn set_empty() -> u32;
                    /// 46 — the canonical empty set
                    fn set_insert(s: u32, elem: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 47 — s with elem (consumes s, elem)
                    fn set_contains(s: u32, elem: u32) -> bool;
                    /// 48 — total membership test (borrows s + elem)
                    fn set_remove(s: u32, elem: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 49 — s without elem (consumes s; borrows elem)
                    fn set_size(s: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 50 — element count (O(1))
                    fn set_iter(s: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 51 — cursor at the first element (or exhausted)
                    fn set_iter_next(cur: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 52 — advance (consumes cur, returns new cursor)
                    fn set_iter_elem(cur: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 53 — current element, or NULL if exhausted
                    /// ── Debug leak oracle (index 54). Returns the number of live heap objects (allocated and not yet
                    ///    freed). This is a VERIFICATION AID, not part of the language: the compiler NEVER imports or
                    ///    emits a call to it (it is absent from the compiler's allow-list, so the envelope and every
                    ///    emitted program are unchanged), and it composes as an extra export the program ignores
                    ///    (component-model width subtyping). A leak-check harness reads it after `run()` and asserts it
                    ///    is 0 to prove the compiler's Perceus dup/drop discipline balances. The live counter is compiled
                    ///    ONLY under the `debug-counters` cargo feature; in the default (shipped) build the counter is
                    ///    absent and this returns 0 at zero cost, so the runtime stays effectively unchanged.
                    fn live_objects() -> u32;
                    /// 54 — live heap-object count (0 unless debug)
                    /// ── Vector concat (RRB relaxed radix, index 55) — O(log N) concatenation. Joining two persistent
                    ///    vectors marks the boundary node(s) RELAXED with a per-child cumulative size table (u32 LE in
                    ///    the tagless node's `raw`, one entry per child), so the radix↔RRB distinction is an INTERNAL
                    ///    representation concern: a concatenated vector is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a push-built one by
                    ///    `vec-len`/`vec-get` (and remains valid for further `vec-push`/`vec-update`). Ownership
                    ///    (value-heap-runtime.md §Constructors Consume And Accessors Borrow): a CONSTRUCTOR — CONSUMES both operands and produces a NEW
                    ///    owned vector (a caller keeping either version `dup`s it first). An empty operand is the identity
                    ///    (`concat(empty, b) == b`, `concat(a, empty) == a`).
                    fn vec_concat(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 55 — a then b (consumes both)
                    /// ── Vector split (RRB relaxed radix, index 56) — O(log N) split by index. `vec-split(v, index)`
                    ///    returns `(left, right)` where `left` is elements `[0, index)` and `right` is `[index, len)`.
                    ///    Trimming the boundary spine marks the affected interior nodes RELAXED (a per-child cumulative
                    ///    size table in `raw`), so both halves are valid persistent vectors indistinguishable from
                    ///    push-built ones by `vec-len`/`vec-get` and remain valid for further push/update/concat.
                    ///    Ownership (value-heap-runtime.md §Constructors Consume And Accessors Borrow): a CONSTRUCTOR — CONSUMES `v` and produces TWO
                    ///    new owned vectors (a caller keeping `v` `dup`s it first). Boundary: `index == 0` → `(empty, v)`,
                    ///    `index >= len` → `(v, empty)`. The pair crosses as a WIT `tuple<u32, u32>` (flattened to two
                    ///    return values by the component ABI).
                    fn vec_split(v: u32, index: u32) -> (u32, u32);
                    /// 56 — (left, right) at index (consumes v)
                    /// ── Set algebra (CHAMP, indices 57–59) — structural union / intersection / difference over the
                    ///    persistent set. Correctness-first over insert+contains+iterate (a recursive CHAMP node-merge
                    ///    sharing whole shared subnodes would be the O(min) form — deferred as an optimization); the
                    ///    result is CANONICAL by construction (built via `set-insert`, so byte-identical `champ_eq`/
                    ///    `champ_hash` regardless of build order). Ownership (value-heap-runtime.md §Constructors Consume And Accessors Borrow): all
                    ///    three are CONSTRUCTORS — they CONSUME both operands and produce a NEW owned set (a caller
                    ///    keeping an operand `dup`s it first). Identities: `union(empty,b)=b`, `union(a,empty)=a`,
                    ///    `intersection(_,empty)=empty`, `difference(a,empty)=a`, `difference(empty,_)=empty`.
                    fn set_union(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 57 — elements in a OR b (consumes both)
                    fn set_intersection(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 58 — elements in a AND b (consumes both)
                    fn set_difference(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 59 — elements in a but NOT b (consumes both)
                    /// ── Bulk vector construct (index 60) — build a persistent vector from an already-built flat `arr`
                    ///    (the tuple/record array primitive) in ONE call, the lowering target for a `(list e0 … e{N-1})`
                    ///    literal: the compiler emits `arr-alloc N` + N× `arr-set` (which it already does trivially) then a
                    ///    single `vec-of-arr`, instead of `vec-empty` + N× `vec-push` (N persistent-trie CONSTRUCTORS, each
                    ///    consuming+rebuilding the whole vector). Ownership (value-heap-runtime.md §Constructors Consume And
                    ///    Accessors Borrow): a CONSTRUCTOR — CONSUMES `arr` and produces a NEW owned vector holding the
                    ///    same elements in order; the result is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a push-built vector by
                    ///    `vec-len`/`vec-get` and valid for further `vec-push`/`vec-update`/`vec-concat`/`vec-split`.
                    ///    Zero-copy for the common case: a ≤32-element list fits ONE trie leaf, so the arr node IS that
                    ///    leaf (reused by move — only the 8-byte header node is allocated); a larger list fills the trie in
                    ///    one pass. `arr-len 0` (the empty array is inline unit) yields the empty vector.
                    fn vec_of_arr(arr: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 60 — vector from a flat arr (consumes arr)
                    /// ── Structural value equality (index 61) — the deep heap walk behind the language's `=` on two
                    ///    RUNTIME compound values (a sum/tuple/record/list whose contents came from a parameter or a
                    ///    call, so neither side is a compile-time constant the compiler folds). `value-eq(a, b)` is the
                    ///    SAME tagless structural comparison the map/set key path already runs (`champ_eq`): two values
                    ///    are equal iff same shape AND equal component-wise (core-semantics.md §Equality Is Structural),
                    ///    with a variant's discriminant compared before its payload — no type tag, no serialization, no
                    ///    upcall. It is correct for ANY value form because every form is canonical modulo the
                    ///    Bytes-rope compaction the compiler already emits before a Bytes key (deterministic-value-form.md
                    ///    §A Value Has One Canonical Byte Form). Ownership (value-heap-runtime.md §Constructors Consume
                    ///    And Accessors Borrow): an INSPECTOR — BORROWS both operands (like `set-contains`), returning a
                    ///    scalar without changing either reference count; a caller that built an operand as a temporary
                    ///    drops it after the compare (the compiler emits the `drop`). Identical handles (structural
                    ///    sharing) short-circuit true.
                    fn value_eq(a: u32, b: u32) -> bool;
                    /// 61 — structural equality (borrows both)
                    /// ── Value-form encode (index 62) — render a RUNTIME value to its canonical binary-AST value-form
                    ///    document (the `codec` format: header · leaf pool · struct table · root), the bytes a compound
                    ///    result crosses the host boundary as. The fixed hole-templates the compiler bakes handle a value
                    ///    of FIXED shape; a RUNTIME RECURSIVE sum (a linked list, a tree) has unbounded depth and no fixed
                    ///    template, so its escape declined. `value-encode(v, desc)` walks `v` to the document, guided by a
                    ///    SHAPE DESCRIPTOR `desc` (a Bytes handle the compiler bakes as a constant — a shape table with a
                    ///    self-`Ref` closing the recursion). The runtime stays NOMINAL-AGNOSTIC: every NAME (`:`, a variant
                    ///    head, `tuple`, `unit`, the type name) is read from the descriptor, never invented; the runtime
                    ///    owns only document ASSEMBLY (leaf dedup, struct indices, byte layout). Ownership: an INSPECTOR of
                    ///    `v` (BORROWS it — the caller/escape owns the drop) and of `desc` (a constant it does not consume);
                    ///    returns a fresh owned Bytes. Traps never — a malformed descriptor or unrenderable shape returns
                    ///    the empty Bytes (the compiler only ever bakes a well-formed descriptor). See
                    ///    DESIGN-recursive-sum-escape-walker.md.
                    fn value_encode(v: u32, desc: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 62 — canonical value-form Bytes (borrows v+desc)
                    /// ── Float32 leaf (indices 63–64) — a Float32 value boxed in its NATURAL 4-byte form, distinct from
                    ///    the 8-byte `box-float`/`get-float` (Float64). A Float32 needs its own leaf so its canonical byte
                    ///    form (and value-encode's shortest-decimal render) is the f32's, NOT a promoted f64's: `0.1f32`
                    ///    stores the f32 bits and renders `0.1`, where promoting to f64 would render `0.10000000149011612`
                    ///    (numeric-model.md §Floating-Point Equality Follows The Canonical Byte Form — each width has its
                    ///    own canonical form). The compiler emits `box-float32` where a Float32 enters a value-heap slot (a
                    ///    tuple/sum/list element, a map key/value) and `get-float32` reading it back. Every NaN canonicalizes
                    ///    to the one canonical quiet `f32` NaN on construction (the f32 twin of `box-float`'s NaN normalize).
                    fn box_float32(v: f32) -> u32;
                    /// 63 — box a Float32 (4-byte leaf, NaN-canonical)
                    fn get_float32(handle: u32) -> f32;
                    /// 64 — read a Float32 back
                    /// ── Arbitrary-precision integer (BigInt, indices 65–71) — a sign-magnitude limb-array LEAF (the
                    ///    `Bytes`-leaf shape: raw bytes, zero handles), ALWAYS heap (never a fixnum immediate — a BigInt is a
                    ///    DISTINCT type from a fixed-width int). Backed by the `bigint::Big` limb library. The compiler emits
                    ///    these for RUNTIME-valued BigInt (a constant folds in the compiler via num-bigint and never calls
                    ///    them). `bigint-of-i64` widens a fixed-width int in; `bigint-to-i64-checked` narrows back, trapping
                    ///    out of range; add/sub/mul never trap (the range grows); `bigint-div` truncates toward zero and
                    ///    traps on a zero divisor; `bigint-cmp` is the three-way compare `<`/`=`/`>` lower to
                    ///    (numeric-model.md §Arbitrary Precision; options/numeric-model/explicit-checked.md).
                    fn bigint_of_i64(v: i64) -> u32;
                    /// 65 — widen a fixed-width int into a BigInt leaf
                    fn bigint_to_i64_checked(handle: u32) -> i64;
                    /// 66 — checked narrow back (traps out of range)
                    fn bigint_add(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 67 — a + b (never traps for magnitude)
                    fn bigint_sub(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 68 — a - b
                    fn bigint_mul(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 69 — a * b
                    fn bigint_div(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 70 — a / b truncating (traps on zero divisor)
                    fn bigint_cmp(a: u32, b: u32) -> i64;
                    /// 71 — three-way compare: -1 / 0 / 1
                    /// ── Vector drop-prefix (index 72) — `vec-drop(v, index)` returns the TAIL `[index, len)` as one
                    ///    vector, dropping the prefix `[0, index)`. It is `vec-split` keeping only the right half (the left
                    ///    is reclaimed), so a SINGLE-u32 result (no tuple retarea) — the shape a list REST pattern binder
                    ///    `(list p… .. rest)` needs (`rest` = the tail after the leading count). CONSUMES `v`. `index == 0`
                    ///    → `v` unchanged; `index >= len` → the empty vector. APPENDED after the bigint ops (frozen-contract
                    ///    APPEND-only rule — a new op goes at the end so no existing op's index shifts).
                    fn vec_drop(v: u32, index: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 72 — tail [index, len) (consumes v)
                    /// ── BigInt remainder (index 73) — `bigint-rem(a, b)` = `a % b`, the remainder of truncating division
                    ///    (sign of the DIVIDEND, the companion of `bigint-div`'s quotient). Traps on a zero divisor. Backed
                    ///    by the SAME `divmod` as `bigint-div` (it returns both quotient and remainder). APPENDED at the end
                    ///    (frozen-contract APPEND-only rule — a new op goes last so no existing op's index shifts).
                    fn bigint_rem(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 73 — a % b (remainder, dividend's sign; traps on /0)
                    /// ── Exact rational (indices 74+) — a `Rational` is a NORMALIZED 2-handle node `[num, den]`, each child
                    ///    a BigInt leaf (lowest terms, sign on numerator, denom > 0). `rational-of` builds one from two
                    ///    BigInt handles (CONSUMES them), trapping on a zero denominator; `rational-num`/`rational-den`
                    ///    recover a component as a fresh BigInt handle (for narrowing/rendering); add/sub/mul/div are exact
                    ///    (never overflow — BigInt components), `div` traps on a zero result-denominator; `rational-cmp` is
                    ///    the three-way compare the `<`/`=`/`>` operators lower to. APPENDED last (frozen-contract rule).
                    fn rational_of(num: u32, den: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 74 — normalize (num, den) → rational (traps on den=0)
                    fn rational_num(r: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 75 — numerator as a fresh BigInt handle
                    fn rational_den(r: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 76 — denominator as a fresh BigInt handle
                    fn rational_add(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 77 — a + b (exact, normalized)
                    fn rational_sub(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 78 — a - b
                    fn rational_mul(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 79 — a * b
                    fn rational_div(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 80 — a / b (traps on a zero divisor)
                    fn rational_cmp(a: u32, b: u32) -> i64;
                    /// 81 — three-way compare: -1 / 0 / 1
                    /// ── BigInt-from-bytes (index 82) — `bigint-of-bytes(buf)` reads a Bytes leaf holding the canonical
                    ///    sign-magnitude bytes (`[sign][LE magnitude, trailing-zeros-stripped]`, the `bigint::Big` leaf form)
                    ///    and returns a fresh BigInt leaf. The compiler emits this to MATERIALIZE a constant BigInt whose
                    ///    magnitude exceeds i64 range — one too large for `bigint-of-i64` — by baking its sign-magnitude
                    ///    bytes as a Bytes leaf (`bytes-alloc`/`bytes-set`, like a constant string) then re-tagging them as a
                    ///    BigInt. CONSUMES `buf` (the transient byte leaf is dropped). APPENDED last (frozen-contract rule).
                    fn bigint_of_bytes(buf: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 82 — BigInt leaf from canonical sign-magnitude bytes
                    /// ── Map/Set enumeration (indices 83-84) — yield a collection's elements/entries as a runtime `List` in
                    ///    CANONICAL order (collections-and-text.md §149: program iteration order == the canonical byte form,
                    ///    which is SORTED — NOT the CHAMP hash order the raw cursor walks). `set-to-list(s, desc)` → a
                    ///    `List a` of the set's elements in canonical element-value order; `map-to-list(m, desc)` → a
                    ///    `List (Tuple k v)` of the entries as 2-element tuples in canonical KEY order. `desc` is the
                    ///    compiler-baked shape descriptor (a Bytes handle, read exactly as `value-encode` reads it) naming the
                    ///    element/key shape to order by; both ops REUSE the same sorted walk `value-encode` renders the
                    ///    `(Set.of …)`/`(map …)` value form from, so the observable list order is IDENTICAL to the value form.
                    ///    Both BORROW their collection + `desc` and return a fresh owned `List` handle; a malformed descriptor
                    ///    or a non-scalar (unorderable) key/element yields the empty list (the never-trap total). APPENDED last
                    ///    (frozen-contract rule — a new op goes at the end so no existing op's index shifts).
                    fn set_to_list(s: u32, desc: u32) -> u32;
                    /// 83 — set elements as a List a, canonical order
                    fn map_to_list(m: u32, desc: u32) -> u32;
